1
00:00:01,050 --> 00:00:01,703
Hi, everyone.

2
00:00:02,211 --> 00:00:03,134
I'm Sonu Kumar.

3
00:00:03,852 --> 00:00:07,186
I work in platform and infrastructure
engineering at Peoplefluent.

4
00:00:07,608 --> 00:00:12,238
I have been doing this kind of work for
about 18 years, build system, deployment

5
00:00:12,243 --> 00:00:14,173
pipeline, Kubernetes, observability.

6
00:00:14,933 --> 00:00:18,269
And for the last few years, more
and more of it has been about the

7
00:00:18,269 --> 00:00:20,119
infrastructure AI workload run on.

8
00:00:21,060 --> 00:00:23,919
I want to talk today about
something a lot of platform team

9
00:00:23,922 --> 00:00:25,933
are quietly struggling with.

10
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:28,840
Everyone wants AI in the platform.

11
00:00:30,051 --> 00:00:34,575
Everyone also wants the platform to
stay reliable, secure, and auditable.

12
00:00:35,572 --> 00:00:37,535
Those two goals don't always get along.

13
00:00:37,682 --> 00:00:41,762
So in the next half hour, I'll walk you
through a reference architecture for what

14
00:00:41,776 --> 00:00:43,955
I call a governed AI native platform.

15
00:00:44,541 --> 00:00:45,574
It is not a product.

16
00:00:46,458 --> 00:00:50,821
It is a way of thinking about the
layers, the controls, and the signals.

17
00:00:51,160 --> 00:00:54,871
Near the end, I'll walk through
a production scenario because

18
00:00:54,879 --> 00:00:59,563
that's where the idea either holds
up or fall apart The challenges.

19
00:01:00,936 --> 00:01:04,840
Let me start with the tension because
it explains most of what follows.

20
00:01:05,471 --> 00:01:09,424
Platform team today have two jobs
that pull in opposite directions.

21
00:01:10,602 --> 00:01:12,201
The first one is building for AI.

22
00:01:13,140 --> 00:01:18,377
Someone of the product side want a RAG
pipeline so the p- support assistant

23
00:01:18,470 --> 00:01:20,406
can answer from internal document.

24
00:01:21,417 --> 00:01:24,747
Someone else want to host
a model for inference.

25
00:01:25,725 --> 00:01:30,195
Then a third team wants an agent
then-- that can actually take

26
00:01:30,216 --> 00:01:32,815
actions, not just answer questions.

27
00:01:33,369 --> 00:01:37,482
All of that needs infrastructure,
GPU capacity, vector storage,

28
00:01:37,572 --> 00:01:42,508
model registries, and it need
to be secure because these

29
00:01:42,519 --> 00:01:44,704
workload touch real customer data.

30
00:01:45,910 --> 00:01:48,007
The second job is building with AI.

31
00:01:49,128 --> 00:01:54,681
That is using AI inside our own DevSecOps
workflow, validating deployment,

32
00:01:54,696 --> 00:01:59,770
triaging incident, catching security
drift be-before a human would notice it.

33
00:02:00,946 --> 00:02:02,742
Here is the pattern I keep seeing.

34
00:02:02,976 --> 00:02:05,392
Most teams are doing one
of these reasonably well.

35
00:02:06,169 --> 00:02:10,298
Very few are doing both on the same
platform with the same controls.

36
00:02:10,907 --> 00:02:16,055
What usually happens is you end up
with an AI platform in one corner,

37
00:02:16,114 --> 00:02:20,767
often built fast by a small team,
and the real platform elsewhere

38
00:02:21,767 --> 00:02:26,177
Two set of rules, two set of
dashboards, and two on-call stories.

39
00:02:27,144 --> 00:02:30,594
And the risk lives in the gap between them

40
00:02:31,594 --> 00:02:37,158
So the idea behind this architecture
is easy to say and harder to do.

41
00:02:38,487 --> 00:02:41,858
One platform, one control plane.

42
00:02:43,053 --> 00:02:47,932
AI workload and your regular
cloud-native services are governed

43
00:02:48,881 --> 00:02:52,813
the same way, observed the same
way, and audited the same way.

44
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:58,102
When I say control plane, I don't
only mean Kubernetes API server,

45
00:02:58,149 --> 00:03:02,207
I mean the whole set of things
that decide what is allowed to

46
00:03:02,208 --> 00:03:04,769
run and what it is allowed to do.

47
00:03:06,358 --> 00:03:10,766
Admission policies, identity, the
registry, the GitOps controller.

48
00:03:11,652 --> 00:03:15,466
If an inference service and a payroll
service go through different control

49
00:03:15,469 --> 00:03:17,969
plane, you don't have one platform.

50
00:03:18,548 --> 00:03:21,981
You have two platforms that, that
happen to share same hardware.

51
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:29,328
Policy automation and auditability
aren't a layer you add at the end.

52
00:03:29,438 --> 00:03:31,106
They run through every layer.

53
00:03:31,524 --> 00:03:38,887
So let's take a look at what those layers
are Five layers of governed AI platform I

54
00:03:38,887 --> 00:03:41,693
think about this platform as five layer.

55
00:03:41,713 --> 00:03:44,820
Let me go from the bottom up.

56
00:03:46,418 --> 00:03:49,187
Layer one is the
infrastructure foundation.

57
00:03:50,045 --> 00:03:55,112
Compute, GPU pools, storage,
networking, and Kubernetes itself.

58
00:03:55,871 --> 00:04:00,622
Nobody talks about this layer until it
breaks, and then it is the only thing

59
00:04:00,665 --> 00:04:03,687
everyone and anyone wants to talk about.

60
00:04:04,673 --> 00:04:08,560
The thing that changes with AI is GPUs.

61
00:04:08,893 --> 00:04:14,191
They are expensive, they are scare--
scarce, and they don't behave like CPU.

62
00:04:15,404 --> 00:04:19,053
You can't slice them the
way you slice CPU milicores.

63
00:04:19,399 --> 00:04:25,390
So you end up doing things like dedicated
GPU node pools with taints, so only

64
00:04:25,404 --> 00:04:29,723
workload that asks for GPU and land there.

65
00:04:31,292 --> 00:04:34,197
And on the network side, if you are
running something like Kiln Yam network

66
00:04:34,202 --> 00:04:40,158
policy between namespaces give you a
real boundary between, say, a RAG service

67
00:04:41,167 --> 00:04:42,781
and the vector database it talk to.

68
00:04:44,439 --> 00:04:46,376
Layer two is platform service.

69
00:04:47,604 --> 00:04:54,169
Identity, secrets, your artifacts, model
registry, service mesh, the CIAT plumbing.

70
00:04:55,129 --> 00:04:58,920
The important idea here is that
a model is just another artifact.

71
00:04:59,654 --> 00:05:04,032
If your container images live in a
registry like Harbor with signing

72
00:05:04,033 --> 00:05:07,959
and scanning, your model should get
the same treatment, same registry

73
00:05:07,960 --> 00:05:09,929
pattern, same promotion rules.

74
00:05:11,149 --> 00:05:16,735
Layer three is the AI workload layer,
RAG, inference, agents, the stuff

75
00:05:16,739 --> 00:05:18,276
people actually come-- came for.

76
00:05:19,359 --> 00:05:21,182
I'll go deeper on this on the next slide.

77
00:05:22,667 --> 00:05:27,738
Layer four is the DevSecOps automation,
CI/CD, supply chain security, GitOps

78
00:05:27,739 --> 00:05:29,870
delivery with something like Argo CD.

79
00:05:30,387 --> 00:05:34,552
And this is also where governed
agent live, the AI that

80
00:05:34,562 --> 00:05:36,003
helps operate the platform.

81
00:05:36,898 --> 00:05:40,728
And the layer five wraps around
everything, governance and observability

82
00:05:41,728 --> 00:05:44,967
The last one is the
point I would underline.

83
00:05:46,396 --> 00:05:49,985
In a lot of platform I have seen
governance lives at the edges.

84
00:05:50,659 --> 00:05:54,840
A check at the ingress, a scan in
the pipeline, and that's about it.

85
00:05:55,802 --> 00:05:59,886
That was the mostly fine when every
change came in through a pipeline.

86
00:06:00,340 --> 00:06:01,561
It isn't fine anymore.

87
00:06:01,609 --> 00:06:05,505
An agent running inside the
cluster can take actions that

88
00:06:05,505 --> 00:06:06,808
never pass through the edge.

89
00:06:07,366 --> 00:06:09,743
It can call the Kubernetes API directly.

90
00:06:10,126 --> 00:06:15,258
The governance and observability have
to wrap every layer, not the guard doors

91
00:06:16,258 --> 00:06:21,667
Each layer can be run and upgraded on its
own, but they all follow the same rule

92
00:06:22,667 --> 00:06:27,803
So in AI workload support, so what does
the platform actually have to serve?

93
00:06:28,084 --> 00:06:35,044
Four things: RAG, vector stores,
chunking pipelines, embedding services.

94
00:06:35,664 --> 00:06:39,258
The part people forget is data
lineage and access control.

95
00:06:40,180 --> 00:06:43,404
When the assistants answer a
question using a document, you need

96
00:06:43,445 --> 00:06:48,979
to be able to say which documents,
which version, and what the person

97
00:06:49,008 --> 00:06:50,614
asking was allowed to see it.

98
00:06:51,703 --> 00:06:54,075
The last one catches people out.

99
00:06:54,772 --> 00:06:58,631
If your vector index does not carry
the document's access permission,

100
00:06:59,223 --> 00:07:02,792
your RAG pipeline can leak data across
tenants without anyone noticing.

101
00:07:03,906 --> 00:07:07,272
So permission filtering has to
happen at retrieval time, not

102
00:07:07,305 --> 00:07:09,205
only when document was ingested.

103
00:07:10,709 --> 00:07:15,037
One more technical point on RAG,
changing your embedding model isn't

104
00:07:15,044 --> 00:07:16,700
like changing a normal dependency.

105
00:07:17,472 --> 00:07:21,542
The vector from the old model and the
new model live in different spaces.

106
00:07:21,548 --> 00:07:22,518
You can't mix them.

107
00:07:24,462 --> 00:07:30,421
So an embedding upgrade means re-embedding
the whole corpus, usually into second

108
00:07:30,424 --> 00:07:35,118
index, and then cutting over basically
a blue-green deployment for your index.

109
00:07:35,842 --> 00:07:39,292
Your platform should ma-make
that a standard pattern, not

110
00:07:39,315 --> 00:07:41,818
a weekend project Inference.

111
00:07:42,700 --> 00:07:49,462
A model serving the-- that auto scales
with latency, SLOs, and cost guardrails.

112
00:07:50,253 --> 00:07:55,734
GPU time isn't cheap, and a runway
workload will show up on the bill

113
00:07:55,755 --> 00:07:57,787
before it shows up on a dashboard.

114
00:07:58,683 --> 00:08:00,554
Auto-scaling here is also different.

115
00:08:01,614 --> 00:08:04,676
GPU utilization is a poor
signal for an inference server.

116
00:08:05,277 --> 00:08:10,138
Queue depth or token in flight
tell you much more about whether

117
00:08:10,138 --> 00:08:11,469
you need another replica.

118
00:08:13,132 --> 00:08:13,533
Agents

119
00:08:14,533 --> 00:08:15,416
This is the new one.

120
00:08:15,546 --> 00:08:20,234
An agent is basically a service
account that makes its own decision.

121
00:08:20,496 --> 00:08:24,899
So it needs defined tool permissions,
approval gates, and an audit

122
00:08:24,914 --> 00:08:26,700
trail of what it actually did.

123
00:08:27,348 --> 00:08:32,873
We should treat it at least as
carefully as we treat a service account.

124
00:08:33,088 --> 00:08:34,254
Honestly, more carefully.

125
00:08:36,012 --> 00:08:40,107
And model lifecycle, registry,
versioning, provenance, and an automated

126
00:08:40,128 --> 00:08:42,498
path from experiment to production.

127
00:08:44,068 --> 00:08:47,420
I'll come back to this because
I think it is where platform

128
00:08:47,483 --> 00:08:50,089
team most need to s-step up

129
00:08:51,089 --> 00:08:54,010
Security foundation The rule is simple.

130
00:08:54,145 --> 00:08:59,035
Every workload, AI or not, runs
under the same zero trust posture.

131
00:08:59,972 --> 00:09:04,831
No implicit trust, every request is
verified, authorized, and logged.

132
00:09:05,250 --> 00:09:08,347
Being inside the cluster
does not make you trusted.

133
00:09:09,817 --> 00:09:11,307
Three things make that real.

134
00:09:12,468 --> 00:09:14,694
First, policy as code.

135
00:09:16,181 --> 00:09:20,961
We use OPA and its policy language,
Rego, to enforce admission

136
00:09:20,968 --> 00:09:23,324
runtime and data access policies.

137
00:09:23,535 --> 00:09:27,461
In Kubernetes, that usually
means OPA Gatekeeper sitting

138
00:09:27,462 --> 00:09:29,256
on the admission webhook.

139
00:09:30,366 --> 00:09:35,083
Kyverno is a good option too if y- if
your team would rather write policies in

140
00:09:35,613 --> 00:09:39,672
YAML The important bit isn't the tool.

141
00:09:40,574 --> 00:09:43,651
It is that these policies live
and get next to the application

142
00:09:43,684 --> 00:09:47,473
code, and they go through pull
requests like everything else.

143
00:09:49,032 --> 00:09:52,050
So when someone asks, "Why
was my deployment blocked?"

144
00:09:52,371 --> 00:09:57,734
The answer is a commit with a reviewer
and a reason, not some- somebody's memory.

145
00:09:58,913 --> 00:09:59,842
A practical tip here.

146
00:09:59,859 --> 00:10:03,759
Don't run on a new policy
in enforce mode on day one.

147
00:10:04,083 --> 00:10:05,614
Run in audit mode first.

148
00:10:05,623 --> 00:10:08,401
Gatekeeper and Kyverno both supports that.

149
00:10:09,152 --> 00:10:15,355
Let it tell you what you would have
been blocked for a week or two.

150
00:10:16,232 --> 00:10:20,857
You will always find a legitimate
workload you would have forgotten about.

151
00:10:22,292 --> 00:10:23,781
Second, model provenance.

152
00:10:24,624 --> 00:10:30,161
Model artifacts get signed, and
they carry a bill of material,

153
00:10:31,593 --> 00:10:32,882
just like a container image.

154
00:10:33,540 --> 00:10:38,694
Tools like Sigstore Cosign work for
an OCI artifact, not only images.

155
00:10:39,162 --> 00:10:41,497
So you can sign a model the same way.

156
00:10:43,326 --> 00:10:48,286
Then the same admission policy checks
the signature before run-- anything runs.

157
00:10:48,823 --> 00:10:51,858
We learned this lesson the
hard way with software supply

158
00:10:51,864 --> 00:10:53,297
chain over the last few years.

159
00:10:53,778 --> 00:10:55,628
Models are the next supply chain.

160
00:10:55,646 --> 00:10:58,255
We don't need to learn it twice.

161
00:11:00,017 --> 00:11:05,107
Third one is RBAC at every layer Who
can deploy a model, who can invoke it,

162
00:11:05,122 --> 00:11:07,242
who can retain it, who can override it.

163
00:11:07,463 --> 00:11:11,442
These are four different permission,
and they should be four different roles.

164
00:11:11,461 --> 00:11:15,271
In practice, le- le- lot of
team give one broad service

165
00:11:15,273 --> 00:11:16,941
account to the whole ML pipeline.

166
00:11:17,435 --> 00:11:19,207
That is the first thing I would split up

167
00:11:20,207 --> 00:11:22,671
Observability is not optional.

168
00:11:23,671 --> 00:11:28,291
And I mean something a bit wider
than what most of us grew up with.

169
00:11:28,884 --> 00:11:33,343
Latency and error rate still matter,
but in an AI platform, some of the

170
00:11:33,343 --> 00:11:35,766
most important signals are new ones.

171
00:11:36,026 --> 00:11:40,720
Model drift, token cost, the path
an agent took to reach a decision,

172
00:11:40,816 --> 00:11:42,452
how often a policy says no.

173
00:11:43,156 --> 00:11:45,344
Those are nice to, nice to have dashboard.

174
00:11:45,987 --> 00:11:48,173
They are inputs to the control plane.

175
00:11:48,631 --> 00:11:51,796
If you can't see it, you can't govern it

176
00:11:52,796 --> 00:11:53,910
Continuous observability.

177
00:11:55,601 --> 00:12:00,124
I group what we measure into four
buckets Infrastructure signals.

178
00:12:01,573 --> 00:12:06,839
CPU, GPU utilization, pod health,
queue depth, network throughput.

179
00:12:07,649 --> 00:12:08,395
Familiar stuff.

180
00:12:08,493 --> 00:12:14,824
But now it covers GPU too, and they behave
differently for the NVIDIA hardware.

181
00:12:15,375 --> 00:12:19,511
D-D-DGCM exporter gives you
GPU utilization and memory

182
00:12:19,513 --> 00:12:21,660
info into the Prometheus.

183
00:12:22,655 --> 00:12:24,413
GPU memory is one to watch.

184
00:12:25,012 --> 00:12:28,821
A GPU can show forty percent
utilization and still be one request

185
00:12:28,843 --> 00:12:30,519
away from running out of memory.

186
00:12:32,080 --> 00:12:33,506
AI-specific metrics.

187
00:12:34,652 --> 00:12:40,909
Inference latency, token consumption,
embedding quality, drift scores, and

188
00:12:40,909 --> 00:12:42,786
some proxy for hallucination rate.

189
00:12:43,792 --> 00:12:45,184
I say proxy on purpose.

190
00:12:45,248 --> 00:12:47,693
Nobody has a perfect
hallucination metrics.

191
00:12:48,401 --> 00:12:52,766
But you can track things like how often
retrieval found a relevant document and

192
00:12:52,768 --> 00:12:54,806
how often user corrected the answer.

193
00:12:55,571 --> 00:12:59,966
A trend is still useful when,
when the absolute number isn't.

194
00:13:01,146 --> 00:13:05,774
One warning from my experience: be
careful with labels on these metrics.

195
00:13:06,219 --> 00:13:09,707
It's tempting to label token usage
by user, by prompt, by session.

196
00:13:10,503 --> 00:13:14,457
In Prometheus, that's a cardinal-
cardinality explosion waiting to happen.

197
00:13:15,476 --> 00:13:21,837
Keep the high detail data in
traces or logs, and keep metrics

198
00:13:22,194 --> 00:13:27,483
to things like model version and
tenant Third is security telemetry.

199
00:13:28,391 --> 00:13:31,854
Policy violation, unusual RBAC
access pattern, and an audit

200
00:13:31,854 --> 00:13:34,000
log for every privileged action.

201
00:13:34,464 --> 00:13:36,643
The Kubernetes audit log is gold here.

202
00:13:36,699 --> 00:13:40,424
If you actually ship it somewhere
searchable like open source,

203
00:13:41,223 --> 00:13:42,270
it's very, very helpful.

204
00:13:44,024 --> 00:13:45,750
Fourth one, agent traces.

205
00:13:46,294 --> 00:13:48,606
This is the one I would push hardest on.

206
00:13:49,229 --> 00:13:53,775
For every agent workflow, you want
the full traces, which tool it called,

207
00:13:54,631 --> 00:13:59,083
with what, inputs and what came
back, and where a human stepped in.

208
00:13:59,443 --> 00:14:03,916
OpenTelemetry now has semantics
conventions for generative AI, so you

209
00:14:03,916 --> 00:14:05,804
don't have to invent your own format.

210
00:14:06,453 --> 00:14:11,831
When an agent does something surprising
at 2:00 in the morning, that trace

211
00:14:11,855 --> 00:14:15,484
is the difference between a 10-minute
review and a two-day investigation

212
00:14:16,484 --> 00:14:20,652
So let's talk about agent doing
actual DevSec, DevSecOps work.

213
00:14:21,908 --> 00:14:24,067
There are four places
where I see real value.

214
00:14:24,707 --> 00:14:26,225
Imple- deployment validation.

215
00:14:27,672 --> 00:14:32,219
The agent checks rollout health
against your SLOs before cut over.

216
00:14:33,002 --> 00:14:35,152
That is more than are the parts running.

217
00:14:37,089 --> 00:14:42,830
It is P99 latency still inside
budget, and is the error flat rate

218
00:14:42,837 --> 00:14:44,137
compared to the stable version?

219
00:14:45,808 --> 00:14:46,638
Incident triage.

220
00:14:46,706 --> 00:14:49,747
It pulls the metrics, the logs,
the recent change history, and

221
00:14:49,748 --> 00:14:51,847
it scopes the likely cause.

222
00:14:52,536 --> 00:14:54,461
And this is key.

223
00:14:54,513 --> 00:14:57,683
It escalates when its confidence
is low instead of guessing.

224
00:14:58,072 --> 00:15:02,016
An agent that says, "I'm not sure, here
is what I found," is far more useful

225
00:15:02,016 --> 00:15:03,652
than one that is confidently wrong.

226
00:15:04,947 --> 00:15:05,574
Self-healing.

227
00:15:06,539 --> 00:15:08,897
Remediation, but only
inside approved playbooks.

228
00:15:08,907 --> 00:15:12,172
Restart this, roll back that,
nothing outside the list.

229
00:15:12,725 --> 00:15:16,823
Compliance checks, continuous drift
detection, someone change a setting by

230
00:15:16,828 --> 00:15:20,678
hand in a production, and then agent
notices no longer matches the Git.

231
00:15:21,967 --> 00:15:24,194
It has gated authority to put it back.

232
00:15:25,277 --> 00:15:28,119
Now look at the flow at the
bottom s- bottom of the slide.

233
00:15:29,453 --> 00:15:34,332
Trigger, evaluate, context, policy,
check, execute, audit Notice

234
00:15:34,342 --> 00:15:35,710
where the policy check sits.

235
00:15:35,930 --> 00:15:40,603
It is because before
the action, not after.

236
00:15:42,387 --> 00:15:44,148
That ordering is the whole point.

237
00:15:44,166 --> 00:15:47,880
The agent does not act
and then get reviewed.

238
00:15:48,334 --> 00:15:51,372
It gets checked, then it act,
then everything is recorded.

239
00:15:52,489 --> 00:15:54,135
How do you actually build that?

240
00:15:54,196 --> 00:15:56,939
Mechanically, it is less
exotic than it sounds.

241
00:15:56,977 --> 00:16:01,081
The agent run with his own Kubernetes
service account with narrow RBAC.

242
00:16:01,827 --> 00:16:05,252
It can patch a rollout in the
one namespace, not delete nodes.

243
00:16:05,947 --> 00:16:09,443
Every tool the agent can call
goes through a thin gateway.

244
00:16:09,472 --> 00:16:11,717
The gateway asks the policy
engine, "Is this allowed?"

245
00:16:11,783 --> 00:16:16,074
Before passing the call on, and the
gateway rewrites the audit record.

246
00:16:16,774 --> 00:16:19,942
The model itself never
hold broad credential.

247
00:16:20,324 --> 00:16:22,635
That is the single most
important design choice.

248
00:16:23,466 --> 00:16:26,854
If the model's reasoning goes
wrong, the permission still holds

249
00:16:27,854 --> 00:16:30,219
Approval gateway are
architecture, not bureaucracy.

250
00:16:30,911 --> 00:16:32,701
Which brings me to the approval gate.

251
00:16:33,063 --> 00:16:38,980
I know approval gate sounds like
the thing that slows everyone down.

252
00:16:39,337 --> 00:16:40,676
I would argue the opposite.

253
00:16:41,069 --> 00:16:45,601
When system can redeploy or change
infrastructure on their own, you

254
00:16:45,603 --> 00:16:47,155
n- you need defined boundaries.

255
00:16:47,638 --> 00:16:51,525
The only question is whether those
boundaries are written down and enforced

256
00:16:51,708 --> 00:16:53,351
or whether they live in somebody's head.

257
00:16:54,076 --> 00:16:55,475
We do it in three steps.

258
00:16:57,093 --> 00:17:02,118
One, define au- autonomy
tiers by blast radius.

259
00:17:03,140 --> 00:17:04,430
Tier one is auto-approved.

260
00:17:05,062 --> 00:17:11,048
Restarting a pod or roll back
a canary that only gets 10% of

261
00:17:11,078 --> 00:17:14,086
traffic, small scoped reversible.

262
00:17:15,516 --> 00:17:18,757
Tier two runs, but a human is notified.

263
00:17:19,470 --> 00:17:23,190
Tier three needs a human to
say yes before it happens.

264
00:17:24,005 --> 00:17:27,709
Anything touching shared infrastructure,
production data, or another team workload.

265
00:17:28,543 --> 00:17:33,081
The question I use to chart
action is simple: If this goes

266
00:17:33,107 --> 00:17:34,900
wrong, how many people find out?

267
00:17:35,409 --> 00:17:37,256
And can we undo it in five minutes?

268
00:17:38,531 --> 00:17:41,001
Two, encode that as policy.

269
00:17:41,813 --> 00:17:44,321
The gate condition lives
in version control files.

270
00:17:45,249 --> 00:17:49,534
A rule might read in plain English,
"An agent may abort a rollou-

271
00:17:49,622 --> 00:17:52,442
rollout in its own namespace.

272
00:17:52,475 --> 00:17:54,034
An agent may not drain a node.

273
00:17:54,682 --> 00:17:58,505
Anything labeled as holding
customer data need human approval."

274
00:17:59,161 --> 00:18:02,150
And that is reviewed in a
pull request, same as code.

275
00:18:03,902 --> 00:18:06,492
Three, log every decision.

276
00:18:07,360 --> 00:18:12,624
Approved or blocked, every agent action
writes an immutable audit record.

277
00:18:13,777 --> 00:18:19,430
What it tried, why, what evidence it
had, and on whose authority Once you

278
00:18:19,435 --> 00:18:21,060
have that, something nice happens.

279
00:18:21,079 --> 00:18:24,711
You can widen the agent's autonomy
gradually based on evidence.

280
00:18:24,867 --> 00:18:30,279
If an action has been proposed 100 times
and a human approved it 100 times, that

281
00:18:30,280 --> 00:18:33,236
is a good case for moving it down a tier.

282
00:18:33,752 --> 00:18:37,031
It is a data conversation,
not an argument in a meeting

283
00:18:38,031 --> 00:18:38,965
model life cycle.

284
00:18:39,301 --> 00:18:42,712
It is often treated a- as a data
science problem, and I think it

285
00:18:42,712 --> 00:18:44,524
is really a platform problem.

286
00:18:44,931 --> 00:18:49,041
Three patterns Registered
registry first promotion.

287
00:18:49,297 --> 00:18:53,595
No model gets to staging or production
without a signed validated registry entry.

288
00:18:54,796 --> 00:18:59,903
Exactly how we treat container
images and the registry entry should

289
00:18:59,906 --> 00:19:01,513
carry more than a version number.

290
00:19:02,270 --> 00:19:07,408
It should say what data the model
was trained on, what approved-- who

291
00:19:07,408 --> 00:19:11,669
approved it, and what it need to run,
how much GPU memory, which accelerator.

292
00:19:12,162 --> 00:19:13,822
Keep that last part in mind.

293
00:19:13,862 --> 00:19:15,403
It comes back in the case study

294
00:19:16,403 --> 00:19:17,709
Henry and shadow serving.

295
00:19:17,955 --> 00:19:22,559
Shift traffic gradually with a
automatic rollback tied to latency,

296
00:19:22,582 --> 00:19:24,203
accuracy, and business KPIs.

297
00:19:24,867 --> 00:19:29,855
Tool like Argo Rollout, lay-- rollouts
or K serve handle the traffic split.

298
00:19:30,173 --> 00:19:33,307
Shadow mode is especially
useful for models.

299
00:19:33,344 --> 00:19:37,360
The new version sees real
production traffic, but it answers--

300
00:19:37,847 --> 00:19:39,227
its answer don't go to user.

301
00:19:39,320 --> 00:19:40,609
You compare them offline.

302
00:19:41,202 --> 00:19:45,170
It is the safest way I know to find
out how a model behaves on real

303
00:19:45,171 --> 00:19:48,371
inputs Drift triggered retraining.

304
00:19:49,191 --> 00:19:52,993
The observability signals we talked about
feedback into the retaining pipelines.

305
00:19:53,414 --> 00:19:54,855
That is the loop closing.

306
00:19:55,620 --> 00:20:00,502
But retraining is itself a change, so the
new model goes through the same registry,

307
00:20:00,812 --> 00:20:04,465
same model signing, and the same cannery.

308
00:20:05,163 --> 00:20:07,192
No shortcut because it is just a retrain

309
00:20:08,192 --> 00:20:11,615
Okay, let me step away from
architecture for a minute and share

310
00:20:11,624 --> 00:20:13,257
what production has actually taught me.

311
00:20:13,296 --> 00:20:13,892
Three things.

312
00:20:14,530 --> 00:20:17,104
Governance debt compound fast.

313
00:20:18,724 --> 00:20:20,684
It is technical debt, but worst.

314
00:20:21,152 --> 00:20:25,764
Every AI component that goes into
production without governance add audit

315
00:20:25,768 --> 00:20:31,990
surface and widens the blad- blast radius
of the next incident, and it is much

316
00:20:31,990 --> 00:20:37,188
harder to add control to something that is
already live and that people depends on.

317
00:20:37,675 --> 00:20:41,007
Nobody wants to be the person who
breaks the demo the CEO loves.

318
00:20:42,193 --> 00:20:44,226
Platform abs- abstraction save teams.

319
00:20:45,132 --> 00:20:49,079
When we give team golden path standard
template policy already attached,

320
00:20:49,116 --> 00:20:54,544
observability already wired in, onboarding
a new AI workload goes from week to days.

321
00:20:55,515 --> 00:20:59,192
People don't want to become
expert in Rego or OpenTelemetry.

322
00:20:59,910 --> 00:21:01,656
They want to ship their feature.

323
00:21:02,901 --> 00:21:04,980
So make the safe path the easy path.

324
00:21:05,940 --> 00:21:08,950
The governed way is also the fastest way.

325
00:21:08,960 --> 00:21:13,479
You barely have to enforce anything
Shared ownership is non-negotiable.

326
00:21:14,019 --> 00:21:17,742
Security platform and ML engineering
have to write the standard together.

327
00:21:17,903 --> 00:21:23,954
When each team group writes its own,
the gap sit exactly between them,

328
00:21:24,387 --> 00:21:25,839
and that is where incident come from

329
00:21:26,839 --> 00:21:30,706
I'll walk through a scenario that is very
close to the kind of thing we deal with.

330
00:21:30,804 --> 00:21:33,824
It shows every piece of this
architecture working to-together,

331
00:21:33,934 --> 00:21:35,288
and one piece saving us.

332
00:21:36,360 --> 00:21:41,553
Picture a RAG-based support assistant
running on a GPU node pool in Kubernetes.

333
00:21:41,870 --> 00:21:44,658
It is about nine forty
on a weekday evening.

334
00:21:45,582 --> 00:21:49,942
A team ships a new version of generation
model, the model that writes the answer.

335
00:21:50,794 --> 00:21:57,662
It goes out through GitOps as a canary, so
only ten percent of traffic sees it About

336
00:21:57,663 --> 00:22:00,126
six minutes in, the signal starts firing.

337
00:22:01,264 --> 00:22:06,719
P99 latency on the canary goes from around
380 millisecond to over two seconds.

338
00:22:07,186 --> 00:22:15,994
GPU memory on those nodes is at 96%, and
two of the inference pod get OOM Killed.

339
00:22:17,338 --> 00:22:18,599
Killed for running out of memory.

340
00:22:20,103 --> 00:22:22,444
At eight minute, theRIAZ
agent picks it up.

341
00:22:23,271 --> 00:22:28,641
It pulls the Prometheus metrics, the pod
lo-logs, and the rollout history from the

342
00:22:28,650 --> 00:22:32,470
GitOps controller, and it lines then up.

343
00:22:32,860 --> 00:22:35,863
The latency spike start exactly
when the canary started.

344
00:22:35,874 --> 00:22:39,469
The OOM are only on canary pods.

345
00:22:40,469 --> 00:22:41,780
The stable version is fine.

346
00:22:42,222 --> 00:22:45,849
So its conclusion with high confidence
is the new model version is the cause.

347
00:22:46,949 --> 00:22:51,004
Proposes action one, abort
the canary and roll back.

348
00:22:51,560 --> 00:22:53,118
Now, that is a tier one action.

349
00:22:53,331 --> 00:22:56,882
It is scoped to one
rollout in one namespace.

350
00:22:57,086 --> 00:23:01,188
It is reversible, and it is
in the approved playbook.

351
00:23:01,641 --> 00:23:04,352
So the policy engine
says yes, and it runs.

352
00:23:05,116 --> 00:23:09,571
At nine minutes, the canary go-- canary
is gone and the latency is back to normal.

353
00:23:09,597 --> 00:23:12,072
No user outside the
10% ever saw a problem.

354
00:23:12,970 --> 00:23:14,837
So far, the agent was right, right?

355
00:23:15,862 --> 00:23:17,542
But then it proposes action two.

356
00:23:17,807 --> 00:23:21,668
It notices, notices that three GPU
nodes are still showing memory pressure.

357
00:23:22,199 --> 00:23:28,063
So it suggests gardening and draining
those three nodes to clear the bad state.

358
00:23:28,080 --> 00:23:29,388
That sound reasonable, right?

359
00:23:31,293 --> 00:23:35,050
Those nodes are a shared GPU pool,
another team's production model.

360
00:23:35,837 --> 00:23:38,495
A completely different service
is running on the same node.

361
00:23:39,701 --> 00:23:44,179
Draining them would have evicted that
team's workaround in the middle of

362
00:23:44,450 --> 00:23:48,394
everything, and the memory pressure
was clearing on its own anyway

363
00:23:48,431 --> 00:23:50,023
because the canary pods are gone.

364
00:23:50,170 --> 00:23:54,240
The agent did not know any
of that, but the policy did.

365
00:23:54,248 --> 00:23:57,368
Draining a node is a tier three action.

366
00:23:57,993 --> 00:24:00,842
It touches shared infrastructure,
so the policy engine denied it,

367
00:24:01,316 --> 00:24:04,784
and the on-call engineer was faced
with the full evidence bundle, the

368
00:24:04,784 --> 00:24:08,092
metrics, the reasoning, what the agent
wanted to do, and why it blocked.

369
00:24:08,113 --> 00:24:10,515
The engineer looked at it for
two minutes and said, "No."

370
00:24:12,437 --> 00:24:15,848
So nine minutes from the first signal
to roll back, zero error outside the

371
00:24:15,850 --> 00:24:21,468
canary, and one unsafe action blocked
by policy before it even ran The agent

372
00:24:21,480 --> 00:24:26,781
was right about the cause, and the gate
was right to stop the second action.

373
00:24:26,828 --> 00:24:29,585
I think that's what good looks like.

374
00:24:29,800 --> 00:24:31,728
You don't need the agent to be perfect.

375
00:24:31,728 --> 00:24:36,026
You need the system around it to
be designed for when it isn't.

376
00:24:36,827 --> 00:24:41,635
And there is one more part, which I
actually think is the most valuable part.

377
00:24:42,169 --> 00:24:44,969
When we looked at why this
happened, the new model version

378
00:24:44,976 --> 00:24:50,433
simply needed more GPU memory than
the old one, but the deployment

379
00:24:50,442 --> 00:24:52,237
resource request hadn't changed.

380
00:24:52,792 --> 00:24:56,118
Nobody had updated them, so the
fix was a new admission policies.

381
00:24:56,133 --> 00:24:59,903
Now every new model registry entry
declares the memory it needs.

382
00:25:00,292 --> 00:25:04,395
If the deployment asks for less
than that, admission rejects it

383
00:25:05,062 --> 00:25:06,634
before it ever reaches a GPU.

384
00:25:07,874 --> 00:25:09,673
That is the loop closing.

385
00:25:11,125 --> 00:25:12,949
An incident becomes a policy.

386
00:25:13,081 --> 00:25:17,370
The policy live in Git, and the next team
cannot make the same mistake, even if

387
00:25:17,381 --> 00:25:19,313
they have never heard about this incident

388
00:25:20,313 --> 00:25:24,098
So the big question, does
all of this slow you down?

389
00:25:24,133 --> 00:25:24,979
I don't think so.

390
00:25:25,015 --> 00:25:28,046
The tension between moving fast
and staying safe is real, but

391
00:25:28,133 --> 00:25:32,675
it is an engineering problem,
not a culture war Think about

392
00:25:32,696 --> 00:25:34,294
the case we just walked through.

393
00:25:35,663 --> 00:25:39,064
Without the gate, you'd have
either stopped the agent from doing

394
00:25:39,078 --> 00:25:43,036
anything useful, or you let it
drain node on a Tuesday evening.

395
00:25:43,037 --> 00:25:48,554
The get-- The gate is what lets you
say yes to the rollback with no human

396
00:25:48,559 --> 00:25:53,321
in the loop because you know that
the dangerous action is covered.

397
00:25:54,222 --> 00:25:56,418
Good governments take away ambiguity.

398
00:25:56,746 --> 00:25:58,737
Team know what is allowed.

399
00:25:59,209 --> 00:26:02,105
Compliance checks run automatically
instead of in a reviewing

400
00:26:02,106 --> 00:26:03,144
meeting two weeks later.

401
00:26:04,064 --> 00:26:07,270
And there are pre-approved
patterns to build on.

402
00:26:07,334 --> 00:26:11,551
In my experience, team with mature
policy as code ship AI feature

403
00:26:11,553 --> 00:26:16,371
faster, mostly because they spend far
less time recovering from incident

404
00:26:16,390 --> 00:26:18,246
and cleaning up after audits.

405
00:26:19,326 --> 00:26:21,409
The goal was never to slow AI down.

406
00:26:21,723 --> 00:26:23,779
It is to make the speed sustainable

407
00:26:24,779 --> 00:26:25,999
The path forward.

408
00:26:26,665 --> 00:26:31,089
If you look at the steps on this slide,
that is roughly how I see this journey.

409
00:26:31,091 --> 00:26:34,539
We started with manual DevSecOps.

410
00:26:34,837 --> 00:26:37,184
Most of us moved to automated pipeline.

411
00:26:37,244 --> 00:26:41,759
A lot of teams are now in the AI-augmented
operation, AI-suggested human acts.

412
00:26:42,154 --> 00:26:46,132
And the next step is governed automan-
autonomous delivery, where AI act

413
00:26:46,132 --> 00:26:47,797
inside the boundaries we have defined.

414
00:26:48,973 --> 00:26:51,497
The word I would focus on is governed.

415
00:26:52,300 --> 00:26:55,686
Autonomy without governance
is just uncontrolled risk.

416
00:26:55,977 --> 00:27:00,085
So my advice, start building the
governance foundation now, before

417
00:27:00,088 --> 00:27:01,976
the agent shows up at scale.

418
00:27:02,675 --> 00:27:06,738
Start small, pick one agent, use
case like rollout validation.

419
00:27:07,357 --> 00:27:10,424
Give its own identity, put
a policy gateway in front of

420
00:27:10,428 --> 00:27:12,427
its tools, and log everything.

421
00:27:13,637 --> 00:27:18,750
You will learn more from what-- one
real deployment than from any amount

422
00:27:18,752 --> 00:27:24,506
of planning, planning Any retrofitting
governance later is much, much harder

423
00:27:25,232 --> 00:27:27,230
and even harder in the live production

424
00:27:28,230 --> 00:27:30,168
So the takeaway, like four things.

425
00:27:30,259 --> 00:27:31,470
unify both mandates.

426
00:27:31,485 --> 00:27:36,449
Build for AI with AI on one governmental
platform, not two separate efforts.

427
00:27:37,704 --> 00:27:39,474
Policy as code is the foundation.

428
00:27:39,482 --> 00:27:44,874
Zero trust, RBAC, provenance,
and audit need to be designed in,

429
00:27:45,087 --> 00:27:49,038
not bolted on after going live.

430
00:27:50,867 --> 00:27:55,023
Observability spans every layer,
from the GPU all the way to the

431
00:27:55,030 --> 00:27:56,730
model drift and agent traces.

432
00:27:56,759 --> 00:28:01,006
If you can't measure it, you
can't govern it Four, approval

433
00:28:01,019 --> 00:28:02,863
gates enable, they don't block.

434
00:28:03,355 --> 00:28:08,792
Including human oversight as policy gives
agent a clear operating envelope, and it

435
00:28:08,809 --> 00:28:13,385
gives your organization the confidence
to widen that envelope over time

436
00:28:14,385 --> 00:28:14,741
Thank you.

437
00:28:15,278 --> 00:28:16,143
That's it from me.

438
00:28:16,188 --> 00:28:17,477
Thanks for spending the time.

439
00:28:17,985 --> 00:28:22,910
If you are working through any of these
in your platform, I would really like

440
00:28:22,910 --> 00:28:26,780
to hear how you are approaching it
and especially where it went wrong.

441
00:28:27,792 --> 00:28:30,109
Those are always the best conversation.

442
00:28:30,176 --> 00:28:31,388
You can find me on LinkedIn.

443
00:28:31,439 --> 00:28:32,049
Thanks again

